As we continue SPSS and introduction in basic&nbsp;
graphics, we should look at scatter plots a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:52.400
common method of looking at associations, or as I&nbsp;
like to think as a way of assessing togetherness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.400 --> 00:37:58.720
in data. In other words, you want to see&nbsp;
what goes with what or more specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.720 --> 00:38:05.840
what variable goes with what other variable.&nbsp;
So scatterplot are a great way of visualizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:10.400
the association between two quantitative&nbsp;
variables. When you make a scatterplot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:14.560
there are some things you should look for. And in&nbsp;
case you're wondering what they are. They include,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:19.440
for instance, whether the association between&nbsp;
the two variables is linear, because a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:23.840
the procedures that are common, assume that&nbsp;
you can draw a straight line through the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.880 --> 00:38:29.120
you want to check the spread of the data,&nbsp;
especially whether the spread changes as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.120 --> 00:38:35.280
go from left to right, on a scatterplot. That's&nbsp;
called heterogeneity of variance and it can cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:40.960
problems with certain procedures. You want to look&nbsp;
for outliers, either univariate. That's a score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:46.520
that's unusual on a single variable by itself. Or,&nbsp;
in this case, what's even more significant is by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:51.360
variant where you have an unusual combination of&nbsp;
scores. And then finally, you want to try to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:55.920
some idea for the correlation or the strength&nbsp;
of the association between the two variables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.920 --> 00:39:01.760
his counterpart will allow you to do all of&nbsp;
those. Now in SPSS, there are three general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:08.720
kinds of scatter plots that you can do. Number&nbsp;
one, is a simple scatter. It's a binary x&amp;y chart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.720 --> 00:39:14.480
easy to do. Number two is a matrix scatterplot,&nbsp;
where you actually have several variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:19.520
and they're simultaneously. And it's a good&nbsp;
way of looking at complex associations between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.520 --> 00:39:26.480
collections of variables. And number three, SPSS&nbsp;
is able to do a 3d scatterplot. But I'll have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:32.480
words to say about that a little bit later. But&nbsp;
