01:12:19.440 --> 01:12:23.040
When you're working in SPSS,&nbsp;
and you're accessing data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.040 --> 01:12:28.720
one of the most important things you can do is&nbsp;
to create labels and definitions for your data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:29.680 --> 01:12:33.920
I like to think of this as the statistical&nbsp;
version of Alice in Wonderland and the caterpillar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:34.480 --> 01:12:40.800
asking her to explain herself, you need to explain&nbsp;
yourself or more specifically, when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:40.800 --> 01:12:49.760
your data, you need to tell SPSS, what do your&nbsp;
data mean. Now, that is the data description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.760 --> 01:12:55.520
and I see two kinds of information that you&nbsp;
tell SPSS about your data. The first one I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:55.520 --> 01:13:01.520
going to call semiotics, which comes from the&nbsp;
study of meaning. This is where you tell SPSS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:02.240 --> 01:13:08.000
what the variable names are the data types,&nbsp;
the variable labels, the value labels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:08.000 --> 01:13:14.720
the missing values, the level of measurement, and&nbsp;
the role that each variable plays. contracted with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:14.720 --> 01:13:20.960
that there are other elements that even call&nbsp;
aesthetics. And that addresses variable width,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.960 --> 01:13:28.400
decimal places, column width, and alignment. And&nbsp;
these are all settings within the data window of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:28.400 --> 01:13:33.840
SPSS. One of the most important though, at&nbsp;
least for human consumption is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:33.840 --> 01:13:38.000
the variable and value labels. And so I'm going&nbsp;
to take a little time and talk about those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:38.880 --> 01:13:43.200
with the variable names. That's what the short&nbsp;
names the ones that you have there at the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:43.200 --> 01:13:47.440
of the column, there are some important&nbsp;
rules. So the rules for variable names.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.080 --> 01:13:53.040
Number one, the names must be unique. No two&nbsp;
variables can have the same name, that shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:53.040 --> 01:13:58.720
be too surprising. It's an identifier. Rule&nbsp;
number two, the names must start with a letter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.720 --> 01:14:04.160
I put an asterisk there because you can&nbsp;
start with an ad a pound sign or $1 sign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.160 --> 01:14:08.880
but you don't want to because those are generally&nbsp;
reserved for special functions within SPSS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:10.880 --> 01:14:17.040
Rule number three names can use letters, upper or&nbsp;
lowercase, they can use numbers, and they can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:17.600 --> 01:14:23.280
period underscore at pound dollar sign, on the&nbsp;
other hand, don't end with a period that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:27.760
cause confusion with the command Terminator. And&nbsp;
don't end with an underscore because that's used&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.760 --> 01:14:33.920
for automatic variable names when SPSS is doing&nbsp;
computations. Rule number four names cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:33.920 --> 01:14:43.440
include spaces. And rule number five names must be&nbsp;
less than 64 bytes. And most text coding systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:43.440 --> 01:14:48.480
that 64 characters, but if you're using a&nbsp;
Unicode system that might be only 32 characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:49.600 --> 01:14:55.840
And the last rule rule number six is the names&nbsp;
cannot be any of these words all and by eq GGTL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:56.960 --> 01:15:04.720
e, lt and E not or two or with Because those&nbsp;
are all reserved function names within SPSS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:04.720 --> 01:15:10.240
so don't create that confusion. So those are the&nbsp;
short names that go at the top of a variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:10.800 --> 01:15:15.760
On the other hand, the label that you associate&nbsp;
with that you can give it a more descriptive name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:16.320 --> 01:15:21.600
Those are the variable labels. And so there&nbsp;
are a few rules for those. Rule number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:21.600 --> 01:15:26.880
they must be less than 256 bytes. That actually&nbsp;
means it could be really long, although you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:26.880 --> 01:15:33.360
usually want to do that, because some procedures&nbsp;
will display as few as 40 bytes 40 characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:33.360 --> 01:15:38.240
and you really want to be able to read what it&nbsp;
is. So you want to keep it short. But you can go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:38.240 --> 01:15:44.160
longer if you need to. Rule number two, the labels&nbsp;
must be enclosed in quotes, although on tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:44.160 --> 01:15:50.320
they need to be straight quotes, the vertical&nbsp;
ones, and not the curly quotes are SPSS chokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.320 --> 01:15:55.760
on those. Rule number three labels can include any&nbsp;
character, including spaces, which is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:55.760 --> 01:16:02.080
that you can't have the variable name, but you can&nbsp;
put it here. So that allows you to put labels that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:02.080 --> 01:16:07.280
sort of float on top of the variable names.&nbsp;
And those can show up in the variable lists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:07.280 --> 01:16:12.880
they can show up in the charts in the output&nbsp;
that you create. Another really important one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:12.880 --> 01:16:19.120
value labels. So you may have a variable called&nbsp;
gender and you may put zeros and ones. But do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:19.120 --> 01:16:23.120
remember what those zeros and ones are. And so I'm&nbsp;
going to show you some ways of dealing with that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:23.760 --> 01:16:28.640
The most important thing is to put value labels&nbsp;
on there. So here are the rules for value labels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:29.280 --> 01:16:35.360
Rule number one, they must be less than 121 bytes.&nbsp;
So that actually is really long, you generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:35.360 --> 01:16:41.840
want to keep your labels pretty short. Rule number&nbsp;
two, like the variable labels, the value labels&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:41.840 --> 01:16:45.520
must be enclosed in quotes, and they need to&nbsp;
be the straight quotes and not curly quotes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:46.800 --> 01:16:51.520
Rule number three labels can include any&nbsp;
character including spaces, that's good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:52.320 --> 01:16:57.680
This is an interesting one. And rule number four,&nbsp;
the value labels do not need to be unique, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:57.680 --> 01:17:05.280
is more than one value can have the same label.&nbsp;
So you might have the numbers one through nine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:05.840 --> 01:17:12.720
And it could be that 789 all say the same thing.&nbsp;
But they underneath have different code interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:12.720 --> 01:17:18.320
situations where you might want to do that. But&nbsp;
mostly, I want to show you how this works in SPSS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:18.320 --> 01:17:23.280
So just open up this syntax file. And this one's&nbsp;
going to be a little different, cuz we're actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.280 --> 01:17:28.320
not going to use a data file, I'll refer to one&nbsp;
but I mostly just want to show you the syntax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:29.360 --> 01:17:37.360
This index file shows how to write variable labels&nbsp;
and value labels. Now, you don't necessarily have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:37.360 --> 01:17:41.520
to put them all broken down in lines, I do&nbsp;
it because it makes them a lot more readable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:41.520 --> 01:17:46.560
it's a lot easier to see what's going on. The&nbsp;
first thing is the command variable labels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:46.560 --> 01:17:52.000
Because it's an SPSS command, it's written on all&nbsp;
capitals. And then what you do is you write the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:52.000 --> 01:17:58.480
short name of the variable. And then you have at&nbsp;
least one space and then you have straight quotes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:58.480 --> 01:18:05.120
and then the long label. So here, for instance,&nbsp;
I've got vair 01, that would be the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:05.120 --> 01:18:11.680
variable. And then this is its label written out.&nbsp;
And you don't need to have anything after don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:11.680 --> 01:18:17.680
need any commas or question marks or semi colons&nbsp;
or anything. You just go to the next one. Now I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:17.680 --> 01:18:22.400
put it into another line, because that makes it&nbsp;
easy to follow. And I run them all through here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:23.440 --> 01:18:28.400
I'm going to make one important recommendation. If&nbsp;
you have a dichotomous variable or binary one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:28.400 --> 01:18:34.000
has only two possible values, and gender might&nbsp;
fit into that category. Let me recommend this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:34.000 --> 01:18:38.640
that you coded as zeros and ones. A lot of people&nbsp;
use ones and twos, but that gets confusing if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.640 --> 01:18:45.760
coded as zeros and ones, and named the variable&nbsp;
after whatever the one is. Now, when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:45.760 --> 01:18:50.960
male and female, I generally give one to whichever&nbsp;
group I think's going to have the higher score on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:50.960 --> 01:18:56.880
my main outcome variable, so it'll switch around.&nbsp;
But if for some reason, I think that men are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

