to have a higher score on an outcome variable,&nbsp;
then I will call it male and then the label will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.240 --> 01:19:07.840
be are for respondent is male. On the other hand,&nbsp;
if I think women are going to have a higher score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:07.840 --> 01:19:13.200
then I will call the variable female and the label&nbsp;
will be RS female, I would obviously only use one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:13.200 --> 01:19:21.440
of those two. Now here are some other examples.&nbsp;
I tend to give generic names such as variable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:21.440 --> 01:19:27.920
or really just q for question q one q two, and I&nbsp;
use the leading zeros so they store it properly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:27.920 --> 01:19:35.520
in the dialog boxes. And when you're done listing&nbsp;
all of your variable names and the variable labels&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:35.520 --> 01:19:40.320
and quotes, just end with a period doesn't have&nbsp;
to be have a space before it that's leftover from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:40.320 --> 01:19:47.920
earlier versions of SPSS. It's a habit I have. So&nbsp;
you can run this at any time and it will assign&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:47.920 --> 01:19:51.840
these labels to the variables and then they'll&nbsp;
show up in the data file which is nice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.440 --> 01:20:00.480
Next are the value labels. And what you have&nbsp;
here is the first command Which is written&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:00.480 --> 01:20:06.320
in all caps. And then you give a list&nbsp;
of variables to which the values apply.&nbsp;&nbsp;

