01:48:12.400 --> 01:48:18.000
Another important procedure in SPSS when you're&nbsp;
analyzing data is something called a factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:18.000 --> 01:48:24.880
analysis. Now, I like to think of it as looking&nbsp;
at your data and trying to find shadows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:24.880 --> 01:48:29.840
In this picture, what you have are shadows, those&nbsp;
are the black figures that you see, it takes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:29.840 --> 01:48:34.480
moment to figure out that you're looking down and&nbsp;
there actually are people, but kind of sticking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:34.480 --> 01:48:39.840
straight out. And so in this photo, we are going&nbsp;
from a sort of a three dimensional origin, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:39.840 --> 01:48:45.120
the person itself to a two dimensional variation&nbsp;
with the shadow. What's interesting about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:45.120 --> 01:48:50.480
is you maintain most of the useful data, you can&nbsp;
tell that there are people that they're walking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:50.480 --> 01:48:54.400
you can probably even tell some things about how&nbsp;
tall they are, what they're wearing, and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.680 --> 01:49:01.600
What you've done is you've made things more&nbsp;
efficient. Now in the data world, that's called&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:01.600 --> 01:49:08.480
dimensionality reduction, where each variable is&nbsp;
a dimension. And too many variables can actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:08.480 --> 01:49:13.840
be really problematic. You're trying to boil&nbsp;
things down a little bit. And you can think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:13.840 --> 01:49:21.200
about the saying less is more or less equals&nbsp;
more. More specifically, that is less noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:21.840 --> 01:49:28.480
and fewer unhelpful variables in your data set.&nbsp;
Equal more meaning because that's what you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:28.480 --> 01:49:34.320
trying to do, you're trying to extract meaning.&nbsp;
Now, when it comes to factor analysis and related&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:34.320 --> 01:49:40.560
techniques, I have one very important piece of&nbsp;
advice and that is to be practical. at all points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:40.560 --> 01:49:47.440
you want to remember what is your goal? So what&nbsp;
is the goal? Well, the goal of factor analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:47.440 --> 01:49:54.400
I'll tell you what it's not. It's not an exercise&nbsp;
in analytical purity. You're not there to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:54.400 --> 01:49:59.200
that you know how to go through all the steps&nbsp;
in the approved format. Really, you're working&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:59.200 --> 01:50:05.440
with your data Because you're trying to get&nbsp;
some understanding. So the goal of a procedure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:05.440 --> 01:50:12.160
like factor analysis is useful insight, trying&nbsp;
to follow the rules, do what you can to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:12.160 --> 01:50:17.360
sure you don't make any obvious mistakes. But&nbsp;
remember, you're not bound by the mathematics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:17.360 --> 01:50:23.280
you're bound by what the data tells you about&nbsp;
the people. Another way of looking at that is use&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:23.280 --> 01:50:30.560
factor analysis, or really any other procedure&nbsp;
for us heuristic value. That is, it suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:30.560 --> 01:50:36.080
possibilities to you as you analyze the data&nbsp;
as you're trying to get insight to people. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

